DP  - 2019 Mar 11
TI  - A Patient with GOLD Stage 3 COPD « cured » by One-Way Endobronchial Valves.
LID - 10.3390/medicina55030065 [doi]
LID - 65
AB  - Lung hyperinflation is a main determinant of dyspnoea in patients with chronic 
      obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Surgical or bronchoscopic lung volume 
      reduction are the most efficient therapeutic approaches for reducing 
      hyperinflation in selected patients with emphysema. We here report the case of a 
      69-year old woman with COPD (GOLD stage 3-D) referred for lung volume reduction. 
      She complained of persistent disabling dyspnoea despite appropriate therapy. 
      Chest imaging showed marked emphysema heterogeneity as well as severe 
      hyperinflation of the right lower lobe. She was deemed to be a good candidate for 
      bronchoscopic treatment with one-way endobronchial valves. In the absence of 
      interlobar collateral ventilation, 2 endobronchial valves were placed in the 
      right lower lobe under general anaesthesia. The improvement observed 1 and 3 
      months after the procedure was such that the patient no longer met the pulmonary 
      function criteria for COPD. The benefit persisted after 3 years.
